<h1>Privacy policy</h1>
<p class="date">Last updated 25 October 2026</p>

<p>Letterlight is a journal app for Android. This page explains what happens to what you write.</p>

<h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Your journal entries, moods, letters, replies, routine and practices are saved only in the app's storage on your device.</li>
  <li>Files you attach as sources (PDFs, images, text) are copied into the app's storage on your device.</li>
  <li>A usage log (how many tokens each request used, and what it was for) is kept on your device so you can see what letters cost.</li>
  <li>Your invite code, and the device token Letterlight's server gives you for it, are saved on your device. There is no Letterlight account.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What is sent to the AI model</h2>
<p>Letters are written by an AI model: Claude, made by Anthropic. When you ask for a letter or a reply, the app sends these to the model's provider:</p>
<ul>
  <li>the entry and mood you're asking about, and the earlier letters and replies in that conversation;</li>
  <li>any sources attached to that entry;</li>
  <li>the mentor you chose and the local date and time;</li>
  <li>for routine and practice reminders, the habit or exercise they are about.</li>
</ul>
<p>Anthropic processes this under its own terms and <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>.</p>

<h2>Letterlight's server</h2>
<p>These requests go through Letterlight's server, which runs on Cloudflare. It passes each request to Anthropic, using Letterlight's own Anthropic account, and returns the letter to your phone. This is why you don't need an API key of your own.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The server doesn't store your entries, letters, replies or sources, and doesn't write them to its logs.</li>
  <li>To keep to daily limits and a budget, it keeps counts: a random device number given to your phone (shown in logs only in a shortened, scrambled form), the kind of request, how many tokens it used and what it cost.</li>
  <li>Cloudflare carries the traffic under its own <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>If you take part in the beta test</h2>
<p>From November 2026, a small group of invited testers tries Letterlight for two weeks. If you're one of them:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your invite code is your participant number. Only the person who runs Letterlight can tell which code is yours.</li>
  <li>For each code and day, the server counts how many letters, replies and reminder lines it delivered, and what they cost. It never sees or keeps what you wrote.</li>
  <li>Testers are split between two versions of Claude, to compare them. You won't be told which one you have until the test ends.</li>
  <li>Surveys (Google Forms) and the exit interview collect only what you choose to tell us. Interviews are recorded only if you agree.</li>
  <li>"Report this letter" opens an email with the letter in it. You see it, and can change it, before sending.</li>
  <li>All study data (the counts per code, survey answers and interview notes) is deleted by 28 February 2027. Write to <a href="mailto:hello@letterlight.app">hello@letterlight.app</a> to have yours deleted sooner or to leave the test.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Web pages you add</h2>
<p>When you add a web page as a source, the app downloads that page directly from its website once and keeps its text on your device.</p>

<h2>What we don't do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>No analytics, advertising or tracking libraries.</li>
  <li>No selling or sharing of your data.</li>
  <li>Notifications are scheduled on your phone; no push server is involved.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Deleting your data</h2>
<p>Delete entries in the app, or uninstall Letterlight to remove everything it stored on your device. Clearing the app's storage in Android settings does the same.</p>

<h2>Children</h2>
<p>Letterlight is not directed at children under 13.</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>Questions about this policy: <a href="mailto:hello@letterlight.app">hello@letterlight.app</a></p>

